# paper3-edge-cost.meta.ttl — claim annotations for paper3-edge-cost.md
#
# Each published statistic in the article carries an invisible anchor at the
# number, <!--@c-<id>-->, and is described here as a schema:Claim keyed to that
# id. The metadata lives OUT of the prose for four reasons:
#
#   1. it is a first-class .ttl, so it inherits the repo's existing rule that
#      every Turtle file is validated with rdflib after editing;
#   2. it composes directly with claims.ttl and data-graph.ttl — the three can
#      be loaded into one graph and queried together;
#   3. human drafters editing prose cannot corrupt it. Inline blocks lived in
#      HTML comments, where a reflowed paragraph or a stray double hyphen closes
#      the comment early and silently eats the annotation;
#   4. it survives a format change. The human draft may not stay markdown; an
#      inline HTML comment does not follow it to LaTeX or a word processor, a
#      sidecar keyed by id does. If the anchors are lost in such a move, the
#      claims are still fully described here and can be re-anchored — which is
#      why every claim carries schema:text.
#
# Validate: python3 -c "import rdflib; rdflib.Graph().parse('research/article/paper3-edge-cost.meta.ttl')"
# Checked by research/scripts/check_paper_stats.py

@prefix schema:  <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix cito:    <http://purl.org/spar/cito/> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix pc:      <https://danlessa.github.io/bicycling-energy-model/paper-claims#> .
@prefix dg:      <https://danlessa.github.io/bicycling-energy-model/data-graph#> .
@prefix claims:  <https://danlessa.github.io/bicycling-energy-model/claims#> .

<#c-a3.discretisation.fidelity> a schema:Claim ;
  schema:text "The per-edge cost sits a median +1.8 pp above its own route-level integral at 5 m — the grade-local epsilon and the clamp floor are the entire discretisation gap" ;
  dcterms:type "derived" ;
  pc:value 1.8 ;
  pc:gateSection "3k" ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence dg:o_e72 ;
  prov:wasDerivedFrom claims:assert33 ;
  rdfs:comment "A3's core claim, first measured in Entry 72 on each ride's own profile (no DEM error); the scope decision of roadmap A3.1 resolved as: its own experiment, e72_edgegrain.py" .

<#c-a3.quantisation.length> a schema:Claim ;
  schema:text "The 8-direction lattice inflates the ridden line by 5.4 percent (median len_ratio 1.0543; 1.2737 at n = 4), and on a smoothed chain that inflation is essentially the whole direction-count penalty" ;
  dcterms:type "derived" ;
  pc:value 1.0543 ;
  pc:gateSection "3l" ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence dg:o_e73 ;
  prov:wasDerivedFrom claims:assert73 ;
  rdfs:comment "Entry 73's quantizer (the map-matching procedure of section 2.3) measured on the IGC population; the alpha*dx column accounts for 23.7 of the 26.4 pp at n = 4" .

<#c-a3.dirs.ladder> a schema:Claim ;
  schema:text "eF2 at the deployed base config (IGC 5 m sigma-10, n = 8) reads +12.7 percent vs measured energy, recovering monotonically to 7.5 at n = 128 against 7.7 at n = 1; on raw FABDEM the same ladder runs 114.8 (n = 4) to 27.2 (n = 128) with the penalty carried by noise, not distance — the direction-count error is chain-shaped" ;
  dcterms:type "derived" ;
  pc:value 12.71 ;
  pc:gateSection "3l" ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence dg:o_e73 ;
  prov:wasDerivedFrom claims:assert73 ;
  rdfs:comment "Entry 73 R1. The FABDEM lattice path reads 1.417x the polyline's h+ at n = 8 — per-pixel noise read by a zigzag path, reported as a finding" .

<#c-a3.edge.pin> a schema:Claim ;
  schema:text "eF4L — the per-edge noise deduction at the LEAVE-ONE-RIDER-OUT measured c pin — beats eF2 on all six DEM chains and is the best Dijkstra-deployable cost on FABDEM (41.08 vs 48.23 med|delta%|); the pooled-pin arm (41.62) is kept as sensitivity" ;
  dcterms:type "derived" ;
  pc:value 41.08 ;
  pc:gateSection "3l" ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence dg:o_e73 ;
  prov:wasDerivedFrom claims:assert73 ;
  rdfs:comment "Entry 73 predictions P7 and P10 (registration v4): the pin is measured geometry only AND out-of-sample per rider — each ride scored with the other riders' median c. P7 survives LORO 6/6; the pooled and LORO arms differ by <= 0.5 pp everywhere because sigma-treatment collapses the per-rider c spread (raw fab30 6.3-11.5 m/km, after sigma-30 3.50-3.68)" .

<#c-a3.deadband.residual> a schema:Claim ;
  schema:text "F3 on the raw chain beats eF2 on the sigma-30-treated map on both populations (5.67 vs 9.21 on IGC; 7.01 vs 11.49 on FABDEM) — map pre-treatment is load-bearing but does not substitute for the deadband; its unique share survives at edge grain" ;
  dcterms:type "derived" ;
  pc:value 5.67 ;
  pc:gateSection "3l" ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence dg:o_e73 ;
  prov:wasDerivedFrom claims:assert73 ;
  rdfs:comment "Entry 73 prediction P8, refuted as registered — the edge-grain sibling of the Entries 63-67 unique-share result" .

<#c-a3.portals.edge> a schema:Claim ;
  schema:text "The straight portal deck on the base path helps 860 of 883 span-touched rides (sign p < 1e-4), eF2 11.81 to 11.05 — at sigma 10 the deck and the smoothing do not collide" ;
  dcterms:type "derived" ;
  pc:value 860 ;
  pc:gateSection "3l" ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence dg:o_e73 ;
  prov:wasDerivedFrom claims:assert73 ;
  rdfs:comment "Entry 73 prediction P4 confirmed. H4's registered sigma-30 stacking arm (registration v3, P9) REFUTED the route-grain carry-over: the deck still helps on the sigma-30-treated chain, 802/880 closer, eF2 8.95 to 8.51 — gated in 3l" .

<#c-a3.default> a schema:Claim ;
  schema:text "The deployable default: the deployed grade-local cost (v2Edge's eps_geo) on a sigma-30-treated map at n = 16 directions — med|delta%| 3.31 on the local 5 m survey, 5.05 on FABDEM — with n = 8 as the fast choice (6.57 / 8.52), portals on, and n = 16 the measured med|delta%| optimum on the local survey (0.20 pp from FABDEM's n = 32 optimum); only the signed bias improves past 16" ;
  dcterms:type "derived" ;
  pc:value 3.31 ;
  pc:gateSection "3l" ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence dg:o_e73 ;
  prov:wasDerivedFrom claims:assert74 ;
  rdfs:comment "The maintainer's synthesis of Entry 73's measured cross and Entry 74's eps contest (2026-08-08, 'our recommendation should be eps_geo'): the grade-local eps wins every treated-map cell while flat eps-2 wins raw chains — adaptive recovery and untreated noise do not mix. No site measurement, no engine change: two config defaults move (sigma 10 to 30 incl. coarse sources; n 8 to 16)" .

<#c-a3.divergence> a schema:Claim ;
  schema:text "How often, on a real network, the energy-optimal path departs from the distance-optimal one, and by how much" ;
  dcterms:type "planned" ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence dg:o_e26_detour, dg:o_e26_grid ;
  rdfs:comment "THE mission-level measurement. Objective MO-6 - energy as the planning currency because energy-optimal routing is accessible routing - is philosophical until this exists. Entry 26's ladder and detour data are seed evidence gathered for a different purpose. Note the distance comparison is settled analytically by beta/alpha (69 at the 50 W reference, above 120 as speed falls); only the FREQUENCY on a real network is empirical" .
